# Wildcard DNS, so a captive portal works without a router that can hijack it.
#
# The UniFi/MikroTik path does this in the router's pre-authorisation rules. On a
# consumer router there is no such feature, and the only thing you can usually
# configure is which DNS server DHCP hands out. So: hand out this node, and
# answer every name with the node's own address.
#
# That is what makes portal detection fire. A phone joining the network asks for
# connectivitycheck.gstatic.com or captive.apple.com, gets this node, receives
# the SOS form instead of the 204 or success page it expected, concludes it is
# behind a portal, and opens the form by itself.
#
# Deliberately off by default — `--profile captive-dns` to enable. A capable
# router already does this, and two things answering DNS is worse than one.

. {
    # Answers every A query with the node. TTL 0: nothing should cache this, and
    # a stale answer after the node moves is a device that cannot reach anything.
    template IN A {
        answer "{{ .Name }} 0 IN A {$NODE_IP}"
    }

    # Answer AAAA with an empty NOERROR rather than leaving it unanswered, so a
    # dual-stack phone stops waiting on IPv6 and falls straight through to the
    # A record above. Silence here shows up as a slow, apparently broken portal.
    template IN AAAA {
        rcode NOERROR
    }

    # Everything else — TXT, SRV, MX — gets an honest NXDOMAIN instead of a
    # timeout. There is no internet to reach; say so quickly.
    template ANY ANY {
        rcode NXDOMAIN
    }

    # Locally visible only. There is no upstream to forward to, which is the
    # point, but it means every lookup lands here and the log is the only way to
    # tell "nobody joined" from "DNS is not being used".
    log
    errors
}
